## Molecular Abundances

As noted in Section [1](#sec:intro), the abundance distribution for the molecule of interest is a crucial factor in converting physical conditions into line emission. The focus here is CO. Motivated by astrochemical models (cite: aikawa96; aikawa02), we set a constant abundance $X_\textsc{co}$ in a vertical layer (cf., (cite: qi08)). The lower boundary of that layer is defined as the isotherm where CO adsorbs onto dust grains: $T = T_{\rm frz}$ (cite: vanzadelhoff01). The upper boundary is the surface $z_{\rm crit}(r)$ associated with a critical column density $\sigma_{\rm crit}$, $$\begin{equation}
    \sigma_{\rm crit} = \int_\infty^{z_{\rm crit}} \rho \, dz, 
    \label{eq:scrit}
\end{equation}$$ meant to emulate the photodissociation threshold (cite: vanzadelhoff03; aikawa06).

We defined the abundance structure as $$\begin{equation}
    \rho_\textsc{co} = D_\textsc{co} \, X_\textsc{co} \, \rho,
\end{equation}$$ where the depletion factor for regions outside the abundant layer is a step-function $$\begin{equation}
    D_\textsc{co} = 
\begin{cases}
    1 & \text{if } T \geq T_{\rm frz} \text{ and } z \leq z_{\rm crit}, \\
    0.001              & \text{otherwise}.
\end{cases}
\label{eq:abund}
\end{equation}$$ We adopted $X_\textsc{co} = 10^{-5}$ in these models, roughly the mean value measured by (cite: zhang21) and predicted in detailed evolution models by (cite: krijt20).

## Fiducial Models

We designed three fiducial disk models to guide an exploration of the quality of $M_{\rm d}$ inferences, with the parameters compiled in Table [1](#table:fid_params). These models all consider a $M_\ast = 1\,M_\odot$ star hosting a $M_{\rm d} = 0.1\,M_\odot$ disk, but with different $\Sigma(r)$ behaviors at large $r$ that permit us to focus on variations associated with regions where the dynamical signal of self-gravity is strongest.

![](sigma_profiles.pdf)

Figure [1](#fig:sigma_profiles) shows radial profiles of $\Sigma$, encircled mass $$\begin{equation}
    M_{\rm d}(<r) = \int_0^r \Sigma \, 2 \pi r^\prime dr^\prime,
    \label{eq:Mencircled}
\end{equation}$$ and the (cite: toomre64) instability parameter $$\begin{equation}
    Q = \frac{c_s \Omega_{\textsc{k}}}{\pi G \Sigma}
    \label{eq:toomre}
\end{equation}$$ for each model. These models are formally gravitationally stable ($Q > 1$), with minimum $Q$ values $\approx$ 3. The key variation between models is in the $\Sigma$ taper ($\gamma$): $p$ was fixed, $r_t$ was adjusted to force the models to have the same effective emission size (Section [3](#sec:simobs)).

![](Trz.pdf)

The thermal structures for the fiducial models are identical: Figure [2](#fig:Trz) shows their $T(r, z)$ distribution. The associated parameters were chosen to emulate the predictions from more sophisticated radiative transfer models, particularly for scenarios where modest dust settling (less turbulent stirring) introduces a steeper vertical temperature gradient at lower altitudes (cite: dalessio06). Figure [3](#fig:nrz) shows the volume density distributions for each model (where $n = \rho / \mu m_{\rm H}$) and marks the layer where CO is abundant in the gas-phase, bounded between the $T_{\rm frz}$ isotherm and $z_{\rm crit}$ surface. These layers span $z/r \approx 0.1$–0.3 outside the midplane CO snowline ($r = 40$ au), consistent with ALMA measurements (cite: law21; law22; rich21; izquierdo21; paneque-carreno23). Note that the “pinch" in the $n(r, z)$ distribution near $z/r = 0.1$ is a consequence of the steep transition in the adopted $T(z)$ prescription.

Figure [4](#fig:dv) highlights the residual velocity fields (Eq. [\[eq:dv\]](#eq:dv)). The $\Delta v_p$ contributions exhibit a vertically layered structure, where deviations near the base of the CO-rich layer are slightly ($|\Delta v_p|/v_\ast \sim$ 1%, or $|\Delta v_p| \approx 10$–30 m s$^{-1}$) negative before diverging to much stronger negative deviations for $r \gg r_t$ (in the $\Sigma$ taper, where $\varepsilon_p$ dominates). The flared density distribution reverses the sign of the radial pressure gradient at higher altitudes, transitioning to positive $\Delta v_p$ residuals for $z / r \gtrsim 0.25$ (or higher in the $\Sigma$ taper). In contrast, $\Delta v_g$ increases with $r$ until $\Sigma$ tapers (where the disk potential gradient diminishes) and is always positive. The largest $\Delta v_g$ are at the midplane, but the vertical variation is modest.

